in @code
in @prism
in @glass
in @meta
in @nl

# @code/mirror — mirror's instance of @code at the mirror altitude.
#
# The recognition (2026-06-06, Reed + Alex → Mara): mirror IS code at
# the mirror altitude. Same as @code/rust IS code at the Rust altitude,
# @code/llvm IS code at the LLVM altitude. The substrate already had
# @code as the universal grammar-at-an-altitude discipline; this shard
# names mirror's own membership in that family.
#
# Why this matters for self-hosting (per the butterfly roadmap, Phase 2
# in `roadmap/wip/butterfly-self-hosting.md` §The compile staircase):
# when @code/mirror is the bootstrap's parser, the bootstrap stops
# being load-bearing for parsing. Mirror parses mirror through its own
# substrate-declared @code instance. The previous framing
# `@mirror/syntax` was wrong-shaped because syntax is not a sibling of
# mosaic / cli / spec / store at @mirror — syntax is what code IS at
# the mirror altitude, which means the substrate already had the word.
#
# === naming: why @code/mirror, not @mirror/code ===
#
# Two arguments converge on @code/mirror:
#
#   1. Path-namespace property (load-bearing per
#      [[architecture-shards-as-substrate-source]]): this file lives at
#      `shards/code/mirror.mirror` and that path declares the namespace
#      `@code/mirror`. Same shape as `shards/code/rust.mirror` declares
#      `@code/rust`. The path IS the declaration.
#
#   2. Sibling discipline: @code/mirror sits alongside @code/rust,
#      @code/gleam, @code/llvm, @code/fortran as instances of the same
#      universal discipline. Reading the family flat-namespaced makes
#      the substrate-pull recognition legible ("mirror is one of the
#      altitudes @code knows about"). @mirror/code would isolate mirror
#      as its own sub-family and lose that legibility.
#
# Alex flagged (2026-06-06) that `@X/Y/Z` namespaces may be isomorphic
# to `@Z/Y/X` because composition is order-invariant; the
# path-namespace property pulls the canonical form to the path-shape,
# which lands at @code/mirror here.
#
# === five operations at the mirror altitude ===
#
# Per the prism-as-trait-as-everything discipline, declaring @code/mirror
# IS declaring the five operations on the mirror-source manifold. The
# operation signatures inherit from @code (universal); the altitude
# specialization names what each verb does at THIS altitude. Bodies
# are abstract (the obligation block \) — the bootstrap discharges them
# today; @mirror/compile discharges them in the self-hosted phase.
#
# Spec context: docs/specs/spectral-triple-binary.md §3 (the translate
# template); roadmap/wip/butterfly-self-hosting.md §Phase 2 (parser
# self-hosting); roadmap/pending/phase-2-parser-self.md.
# Substrate decisions: [[architecture-shards-as-substrate-source]],
#                      [[architecture-prism-as-trait-as-everything]],
#                      [[architecture-lift-as-load-bearing]].
# Related shards: code.mirror (the universal parent),
#                 code/rust.mirror (D2; the sibling precedent),
#                 code/gleam.mirror (post-migration from boot/04b),
#                 mirror/store.mirror (where @code/mirror artifacts
#                 settle as content-addressed splinters).
# Path-namespace property: this file at `shards/code/mirror.mirror`
# declares `@code/mirror` and only that.

prism @code/mirror {
  focus mirror
  project mirror
  split mirror
  shift mirror
  settle mirror
}

# === type-declaration grammar — newline-tolerant variant separator ===
#
# Substrate-pull-realize tick (2026-06-06, Reed + Alex → Mara). The
# bootstrap (`bootstrap/src/tokenize.rs`, the Split branch for the
# `type` keyword) currently slurps the body of a type declaration up to
# the first newline / semicolon / brace and discards it; the leading
# `|` of a continuation line then surfaces as a Dark region under
# `--strict`. The substrate, however, has been WRITTEN in the multi-
# line shape from the start: glass.mirror's `type imperfect`, `type
# transparency`, `type verdict`, `type splinter_shape` (lines 213-216,
# 220-223, 233-237, plus the lift-tick additions) all use the
# `|`-leading multi-line form because that's what readability pulls
# toward in the wild. The bootstrap parser is BEHIND the substrate;
# `mirror compile --strict shards/glass.mirror` surfaces 24 dark
# regions today, every one of them a continuation `|` line.
#
# The fix is not to extend mirror's syntax. It's to declare mirror's
# variant-separator rule HERE, at the substrate altitude that Phase 2
# wires as the bootstrap's parser (per roadmap/pending/phase-2-parser-
# self.md task 1: "Write @code/mirror grammar describing tokenization
# rules using the five operations"). The substrate already carries the
# rule's primitive: `charset(whitespace)` (cf. boot/std/mirror/grammar.
# mirror line 21, boot/std/mirror/glass/ast/token.mirror line 72,
# boot/std/mirror/nl.mirror line 14) is the universal whitespace class
# — by substrate convention it admits space, tab, carriage return,
# AND newline. The multi-line tolerance falls out of using that
# primitive as the separator's surround; no new combinator needed.
#
# Said another way: the substrate already had the rule. We just
# hadn't NAMED the inheritance. Same shape as the @code recognition
# itself (mirror IS code at the mirror altitude), as splinter_graph →
# mosaic(@store), as fragment → splinter. The substrate-pull pattern.
#
# Phase 2 will discharge the obligation: when the bootstrap's parser is
# replaced by @code/mirror, the multi-line form parses identically to
# the single-line form, and `mirror compile --strict` over glass.mirror
# / transit.mirror / refract.mirror returns zero darks for the variant
# separators. Until then this declaration is forward-looking; the
# bootstrap stays single-line-only and substrate-pull-honest call sites
# (transit.mirror, refract.mirror) keep the flattened shape to honor
# the bootstrap constraint while the substrate carries the truth.
#
# Path-namespace note: this grammar block declares `@code/mirror/grammar`
# under the path-namespace property
# ([[architecture-shards-as-substrate-source]]). The block belongs to
# @code/mirror as code IS grammar at the mirror altitude (per the
# Phase-2 recognition); the sub-prism naming preserves the Phase-2
# language ("@code/mirror grammar") at the substrate altitude.

grammar @code/mirror/grammar("mirror", "spec", "meta", "glass", "shard", "shatter") {
  in @meta

  # ===== lexical primitives =====
  # Whitespace by substrate convention admits space, tab, carriage
  # return, AND newline. The newline tolerance below is structural —
  # the multi-line tolerance of the variant separator follows from
  # using `whitespace` as its surround, not from a separator-specific
  # rule.
  fixed whitespace        = repeat(charset(whitespace), 0, none)
  fixed identifier        = repeat(charset(word_char), 1, none)
  fixed name              = repeat(charset(name_char), 1, none)

  # ===== type declaration with closed-sum variants =====
  # `type <name> = <variant> (| <variant>)*`
  #
  # The separator is `whitespace "|" whitespace`. Because `whitespace`
  # admits newlines (cf. lexical primitives above), the production
  # accepts BOTH shapes identically:
  #
  #   type property = wall_clock | fp_precision | cache_pressure
  #
  #   type property = wall_clock
  #                 | fp_precision
  #                 | cache_pressure
  #
  # and the leading-`|` shape glass.mirror already uses:
  #
  #   type imperfect(a, e, l) =
  #     | success(a)
  #     | partial(a, l)
  #     | failure(e, l)
  #
  # The variant body is whatever the host grammar admits as a type
  # term (a bare identifier; a parametric application `T(U)`; a
  # tuple). The `until_separator` reader stops at the next `|` or
  # the end of the declaration; downstream resolution interprets
  # the captured bytes as a type term in the same way action
  # declarations interpret their body.
  fixed variant_separator = seq(whitespace, literal("|"), whitespace)
  fixed variant           = until_separator
  fixed variant_list      = seq(variant, repeat(seq(variant_separator, variant), 0, none))
  fixed type_form         = seq(literal("type"), whitespace, identifier, whitespace, literal("="), whitespace, variant_list)
}

out @code/mirror
out @code/mirror/grammar
